Chapter: FilesPrepassCostTest
  cites Works chapter GopFiles
  cites Works chapter GopFat16
  cites Foreword chapter Fat16
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 What a metadata PRE-PASS would cost, against the listing it is meant to size.

 D.5 cannot size a hole for the Files pane, because `want` is what the
 listing allocates and the listing is what produces the number. The ruling to
 test is a pre-pass: read the directory's metadata first, compute `want` from
 it, then enter the hole. **That is only worth building if reading the
 metadata costs materially less than the listing it sizes**, and this chapter
 measures the ratio rather than arguing it.

 THE FIRST LINE IS THE CONTROL. `alloc-bytes 0` advances the bump allocator
 by nothing and answers the frontier, so a difference across a call is what
 that call made permanent. Two reads with nothing between must differ by
 zero, or the instrument allocates and every number below is its own
 footprint (L-FALSIF).

 THE LAST LINE IS THE OTHER CONTROL, and without it a row of zeros reads as
 a free pre-pass rather than as a probe that stopped working: a known 64
 bytes is allocated and the same probe must see it.

 `gfat-resolve` is the metadata walk the desk already has: it descends a
 path and answers one entry, reading the same directory sectors a listing
 reads and building no entry list. It is the closest thing in the tree to
 the pre-pass a caller would write, so its cost is the honest estimate of
 one, and its RATIO to the listing is the number the ruling turns on.

 A PRE-PASS THAT COSTS WHAT THE LISTING COSTS BUYS NOTHING, because the hole
 it sizes saves the pane's bytes ONCE, on a reopen after a buried close, while
 the pre-pass spends its own on EVERY open including the ordinary first one.

 THE "UNDER A TENTH" ROW IS A THRESHOLD THE MEASUREMENT DOES NOT MEET, and its
 FAIL is the verdict rather than a broken arm: the walk costs about two thirds
 of the listing. Worse for D.5 specifically, the walk's bytes are allocated
 BEFORE the hole is entered, so they land at the frontier, which is the growth
 D.5 exists to stop. The row is kept failing on purpose; softening it to a
 threshold the number happens to meet would be an instrument describing
 nothing (L-INSTRUMENT).

Section: Body

  fpc-yn : Boolean -> Text
  fpc-yn (v) = if v then "PASS" else "FAIL"

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, Device.Port] Nothing = act
    let c0 = alloc-bytes 0
    in let c1 = alloc-bytes 0
    in act
      print-line-uni ("CONTROL, the probe allocates nothing: " & fpc-yn (c1 - c0 == 0))
      m <- gfat-mount-esp
      if m.gm-ok == False then print-line-uni "mount FAILED"
      else act
        let flag = if gfat-is32 (m.gm-vol) then (m.gm-vol).vol-root-start else 0 - 1
        in let p0 = alloc-bytes 0
        in act
          d <- gfat-resolve (m.gm-vol) "EFI"
          let p1 = alloc-bytes 0
          in act
            e1 <- gfl-list-cur (m.gm-vol) [flag]
            let p2 = alloc-bytes 0
            in act
              print-line-uni ("a metadata walk allocates: " & integer-to-text (p1 - p0))
              print-line-uni ("the listing it would size allocates: " & integer-to-text (p2 - p1))
              print-line-uni ("root entries: " & integer-to-text (list-length e1))
              print-line-uni ("the walk is cheaper than the listing: " & fpc-yn (p1 - p0 < p2 - p1))
              print-line-uni ("the walk is under a tenth of it: " & fpc-yn ((p1 - p0) * 10 < p2 - p1))
              let b0 = alloc-bytes 0
              in let burn = alloc-bytes 64
              in let b1 = alloc-bytes 0
              in print-line-uni ("CONTROL, the probe can see 64: " & fpc-yn (b1 - b0 >= 64))
            end
          end
        end
      end
    end
  end
